diff --git a/platform/scheduling/require-availability.yaml b/platform/scheduling/require-availability.yaml
deleted file mode 100644
index 97a941976..000000000
--- a/platform/scheduling/require-availability.yaml
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,149 +0,0 @@
-# crew#555 / idp#545. The CI gate (bin/idp-availability-gate) refuses a PR that drops a
-# founder-facing surface below the standard. This is the same standard at admission, so a
-# `kubectl apply`, a chart upgrade or a hand edit that never sees a PR is refused by the cluster
-# itself -- configuration auditing before the outage, not runtime monitoring after it.
-#
-# Scope is a namespace label, not a name list: a namespace carrying
-# `availability.idp/tier: founder-facing` is enforced, and bin/idp-availability-gate FAILS any
-# namespace whose surfaces it graded green that does not carry the label. So the two cannot drift:
-# CI proves the label is on every namespace the cluster must enforce.
-#
-# StatefulSets are deliberately out of scope: every database here is one instance by design and
-# docs/policy/availability-standard.md says so out loud rather than pretending otherwise.
-apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
-kind: ClusterPolicy
-metadata:
-  name: require-availability
-  annotations:
-    policies.kyverno.io/title: Founder-facing workloads survive losing one node
-    policies.kyverno.io/category: crew#555 availability standard
-    policies.kyverno.io/severity: critical
-    policies.kyverno.io/subject: Deployment, PodDisruptionBudget
-    policies.kyverno.io/description: >-
-      2026-08-28 06:20-06:39Z: cordoning one node put the only catalogue pod into Pending and
-      the catalogue hostname answered 503 for 15 minutes (the zone is a cluster row, never a
-      literal here). Nothing was broken -- the
-      manifest said replicas 1 and platform/ held no PodDisruptionBudget at all. These rules make
-      that manifest impossible to admit.
-spec:
-  background: true
-  rules:
-    - name: founder-facing-runs-two
-      match:
-        any:
-          - resources:
-              kinds: [Deployment]
-              namespaceSelector:
-                matchLabels: { availability.idp/tier: founder-facing }
-      validate:
-        failureAction: Enforce
-        message: >-
-          crew#555: a founder-facing Deployment runs at least 2 replicas. One pod is one node
-          event away from a 503 (the catalogue outage of 2026-08-28 06:20Z). If this workload
-          genuinely cannot be two (one token, one RWO volume), it belongs in
-          platform/availability.yaml with an open issue, not at replicas 1 in a founder-facing
-          namespace.
-        pattern:
-          spec:
-            replicas: ">1"
-    - name: founder-facing-spreads-across-nodes
-      match:
-        any:
-          - resources:
-              kinds: [Deployment]
-              namespaceSelector:
-                matchLabels: { availability.idp/tier: founder-facing }
-      validate:
-        failureAction: Enforce
-        message: >-
-          crew#555: two replicas on one node are one replica. Needs a required podAntiAffinity or
-          a DoNotSchedule topologySpreadConstraint on kubernetes.io/hostname.
-        anyPattern:
-          - spec:
-              template:
-                spec:
-                  affinity:
-                    podAntiAffinity:
-                      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
-                        - topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
-          - spec:
-              template:
-                spec:
-                  topologySpreadConstraints:
-                    - topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
-                      whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
-    - name: a-pdb-never-refuses-every-drain
-      match:
-        any:
-          - resources:
-              kinds: [PodDisruptionBudget]
-      validate:
-        failureAction: Enforce
-        message: >-
-          crew#555: this budget leaves the drain no move at all. maxUnavailable 0 (or a
-          minAvailable of 100%) is what the clickhouse budget did to the drain at 06:30:43Z on
-          2026-08-28: kubectl drain retried and gave up. State a budget that permits one eviction.
-        deny:
-          conditions:
-            any:
-              - key: "{{ request.object.spec.maxUnavailable || '' }}"
-                operator: AnyIn
-                value: [0, "0", "0%"]
-              - key: "{{ request.object.spec.minAvailable || '' }}"
-                operator: AnyIn
-                value: ["100%"]
-    # crew#307, 2026-08-28. The rule above (founder-facing-spreads-across-nodes) MANDATES a
-    # required podAntiAffinity on kubernetes.io/hostname. This rule is its other half, and its
-    # absence is why the portal went stale for seventeen hours: the estate forced every
-    # founder-facing Deployment into the shape that cannot surge, and then said nothing about
-    # the update strategy such a Deployment needs. catalogue carried maxSurge 1 /
-    # maxUnavailable 0 on a two-node pool -- a surge asks for a third pod, hard hostname
-    # anti-affinity forbids placing it beside either replica, and maxUnavailable 0 forbids
-    # freeing one first. Impossible arithmetic, admitted without a word, and SILENT: the old
-    # ReplicaSet is never touched, so the site answers 200 while serving yesterday.
-    #
-    # Unset is refused, not just literal zero. apps/v1 defaults maxUnavailable AND maxSurge to
-    # 25%, and ResolveFenceposts rounds maxUnavailable DOWN and maxSurge UP -- at replicas 2
-    # that is (0, 1), the incident reached by typing nothing at all. A policy that refused only
-    # the literal 0 would be a policy the next ordinary manifest walks straight past.
-    - name: a-rollout-can-always-free-a-node
-      match:
-        any:
-          - resources:
-              kinds: [Deployment]
-      preconditions:
-        all:
-          # Only Deployments that require a node of their own can deadlock. Everything else may
-          # surge onto any node and rolls fine at maxUnavailable 0 -- LAW 38, a guard that
-          # refuses correct work is an outage, and most of this estate looks like that.
-          - key: "{{ length(request.object.spec.template.spec.affinity.podAntiAffinity.requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution[?topologyKey=='kubernetes.io/hostname'] || `[]`) }}"
-            operator: GreaterThan
-            value: 0
-          # Recreate deletes every old pod before creating a new one, so it never surges and
-          # never deadlocks. Its downtime is a different policy's business.
-          - key: "{{ request.object.spec.strategy.type || 'RollingUpdate' }}"
-            operator: NotEquals
-            value: Recreate
-      validate:
-        failureAction: Enforce
-        message: >-
-          crew#307: this Deployment requires a node of its own (podAntiAffinity required on
-          kubernetes.io/hostname) and its rolling update can free no pod, so the surge pod needs
-          a node no replica occupies and no manifest can promise one. It will sit Pending for
-          ever while the old ReplicaSet keeps serving -- an outage that answers 200. Set
-          spec.strategy.rollingUpdate.maxUnavailable to at least 1 and maxSurge to 0. Leaving
-          the strategy unset is the same bug: apps/v1 defaults it to 25%, which rounds down to 0
-          at two replicas.
-        deny:
-          conditions:
-            any:
-              - key: "{{ request.object.spec.strategy.rollingUpdate.maxUnavailable || 'unset' }}"
-                operator: AnyIn
-                value: [0, "0", "0%", "unset"]
-    # There is deliberately NO rule here asserting that a PodDisruptionBudget exists. The obvious
-    # shape -- a context apiCall listing poddisruptionbudgets in the namespace -- panics the
-    # Kyverno CLI 1.19.0 that bin/idp-kyverno-render and CI run ("you must register resource to
-    # list kind ... Resource=poddisruptionbudgets"), so shipping it would take the estate's own
-    # policy judge down on every PR. bin/idp-availability-gate is the enforcing check for PDB
-    # existence and is the better one anyway: it reads the whole rendered directory at once, where
-    # apply ordering does not exist, and it refuses the PR before anything reaches the cluster.
